%
%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Threads: Smaller Units of Computation}
We have talked about:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Programs
	\item Tasks
	\item Subroutines/Functions\footnote{When I use a slash (/) in a list like this I mean that the two terms are equivalent}
	\item Processes
	\item Threads
\end{itemize}
Confused yet??


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
With any complex computing system we naturally break it up into {\it units }.

For example in the EE472 lab exercise:  ``tasks"
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Each unit has

\begin{itemize}
	\item Code/instructions
	\item Data
	\item Context/State
	\item Resources (memory, I/O devices, semaphores)
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*chn>
\begin{center}
%\vspace{2.5in}
   \includegraphics[width=3in]{sched_figs/00264.eps}
\end{center}

<s>\scalebox{1.0}{
<s>\includegraphics{sched_figs/00264.eps} }


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

A {\it Program or Process} is a unit of computation with all of the above attributes.

A {\it Subroutine or Function} is a unit of comptuation with code, data, and context, but
no managed resources.

<*hn> I am using the term ``managed resources" to mean resources which can be assigned and deassigned
by the operating system such as memory or I/O devices.   Of course a function within a program has its
own memory, but if it requests memory from the OS (for example with {\tt malloc()}, the OS assigns it
to the program, not to the function within that program.

<*>

A {\it Thread} is a unit of computation with code and context, but no private data.  Threads may even
share code with each other.

Threads are owned by a program/process.

Threads are like pieces within a program which can be scheduled by the OS independently.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*chn>
\begin{center}
%\vspace{2.5in}
   \includegraphics[width=3in]{sched_figs/00265.eps}
\end{center}
<*>
<s>  \scalebox{1.0}{  \includegraphics{sched_figs/00265.eps} }

A complete software system with two processes.   
<s>Process 1: normal, Process 2: threaded
<chn>Process 1 is a ``normal" single-threaded process.
<chn>Process 2 is a multithreaded process.

<chn>Scheduler manages 6 units:  Process 1 and the 5 threads of process 2.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2.1
\subsection{Thread Context Switching}
Since threads have a smaller context than programs, context switching is faster.



\begin{itemize}
	\item Only save/restore CPU state
	\item No need to change memory setup

<chn>(with programs in systems bigger than the 16-bit 80188, this can be elaborate).

\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Why use threads?

An example.

Web server.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Web server pseudocode:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1)  {
    OS_read_network();      // get incoming request
    compute;                // figure out what html page to get
    OS_Disk_I/O();          // read disk file for www page
    OS_write_network();	    // send page to user
}
\end{verbatim}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Alternative 1:  Run above as a single process.

Problem:  When process is waiting for network, disk is idle and vice versa.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Alternative 2:   Set up several threads of same code:

Advantage:   Scheduler can start a thread which is done with disk I/O while
another thread is waiting on network and vice versa.

CPU is used more efficiently and system delivers more pages per second with same hardware performance.

Potential Problem:  more time spent context switching.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.2
\subsection{Types of threads.}

There are lots of possibilities for size of {\it units}.

The more context a thread contains, the ``heavier" it is.

Heavier threads take more time for context switching.

OS designer determines what kind of threads to support --- application programmer gets only one
option.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 3 
\section{RTOS Scheduling Concepts}
<hcn>RTOS = Real Time Operating System

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{Time}\label{Time}


Time is measured by a piece of hardware which records actual
clock time: a {\it real time clock}:
<chn>We use the terms

``Time Quanta'' ``Time Slice''  ``Ticks''

Let's call the period of time ticks: $T$.

Typically $T < P_{min}$. Sometimes $T << P_{min}$.

<*chn>
Where $P_{min}$ is the shortest period of all tasks in the  system.
In other words, the operating system measures time in units, $T$, which
are smaller than the shortest period of any task.
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.2
\subsection{Soft vs. Hard Real Time}

{\bf Real Time:}  A software system with specific speed or response
time requirements.

{\bf Soft Real Time:} If the deadlines are not met,
performance is considered low.

{\bf Hard Real Time:} A computer system in which at least one task must meet
deadlines in time.  If the deadlines are not met, the system has failed.

{\bf Super Hard Real Time:} Mostly Periodic Tasks:
Task periods = the OS time tick, task compute times ($C$) and
deadlines ($D$) are very
short. $P = D = T, C << P$.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.3
\subsection{Scheduling}

At each opportunity, OS asks:
If $N$ tasks are {\bf Ready}, which one should I run?

If you can show that scheduler always can achieve deadlines, 
the system is ``deterministically schedulable".


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.4
\subsection{Scheduling Goals}
\begin{enumerate}
\item CPU Utilization:
\[
U_{CPU} = 1 - \frac{idle}{period}
\]
Tradional measure for mainframes.  100\% is better.

{\bf But} $U_{CPU} ~= 100\%$ is not safe for real-time systems

Goal:   low load - 40\%, high load - 90\%.

\item Throughput: 
Work units completed per unit time.

\item Turnaround Time:
Total time from task start to completion (waiting + execution + I/O)

\item Waiting Time: 
Choose scheduler which minimizes waiting time. 

\item Response Time **
How quickly system responds to external asynchronus events. 
\end{enumerate}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\section*{Scheduler Types:}
%%%%** Section 3.4.1 
\subsubsection{Periodic Schedulers}
<chn>\begin{itemize}

<chn>        \item
Infinite Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Most primitive of all
                \item Also known as Non-preemptive Round Robin.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly
                \item $C << P$, $P=T$.

\begin{verbatim}
taskN_fcn()
{
compute a little bit;
return();
}
\end{verbatim}
      \end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item
Synchronized Infinite Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item the top of the loop waits on a hardware clock.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
wait(CLOCK_PULSE);
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly, as above.
                \item $C << P$, $T=P$.
        \end{itemize}

<chn>\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsection{Pre-emption}
Each function in these schedulers must ``voluntarily" return back to the main loop, 
and must make sure that it doesn't compute for too much time.   If a task gets
stuck, it breaks the whole system.  

The fix for this is {\bf preemption} - the ability to break into a running task and 
stop it. In other words to force a context switch. 


<*chn>
The schedulers above are appropriate when all tasks are periodic and hard real-time.
However, because execution times ($C$) vary, we have to allow  extra time in
each loop of the non-preemptive round robin schedule (taken up by the ``wait()'' function).

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<*>

%%%%** Section 4 
\section{Scheduling Exercises}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 4.1
\subsection{Overview}

In this section we will conduct a series of in-class exercises.  We will zoom in on exactly how each scheduling method works with a variety of real-time task types.   Our exercises will study the following schedulers (with increasing sophistication):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Our basic {\tt while(1)} loop:  Non-preemptive Round Robin (RR).
  \item The same loop with synchronization timing loop or timer function: Synchronized Non-premptive RR
  \item Special hacks to the synchronized non-preemptive RR to enable different task periods. 
  \item Adding a {\tt sleep(int d)} delay option for tasks: Non-premptive synchronized RR with delays.
  \item Making the scheduler dynamic: Non-premptive synchronized RR with {\tt halt\_me()} function.
  \item Handling I/O requests which take time.
  \item Adding the ability to break into running tasks: {\it Pre-emptive} RR
  \item Allowing for programmer-set priorities among the tasks: Pre-emptive {\it Priority Based}.
\end{enumerate}



\subsubsection*{Diagramming Scheduler Operation}
<*chn>
To study scheduling, print out a ``Scheduler Diagramming Worksheet"
<chn> on page \pageref{SchedWorksheet}.
This chart plots time going down the page and CPU
processes (tasks) across the page. We can think of the horizontal axis
as the memory space of the computer. We will put a shaded box in a
square (or part of a square) when a certain task is running on the CPU.
For example, when the scheduler is running, we would shade under the
``OS'' column. We might also have three tasks labeled A, B, C. We would
shade their columns when they are running. Remember, {\em only one column
may be shaded in each row}.

Assume that each line represents 333$\mu$s.   Assume that there is
a system clock which generates timer events every 1,000 $\mu$s.
To indicate these, make a mark at the left edge of every third
horizontal line.

Relax a bit about exactness or accuracy when doing this kind of diagramming. 
It would be impossible to make an {\it exact} diagram without full access to 
and understanding of the scheduler's code.   The main purpose of these exercises
is to understand key features and attributes of various schedulers as we
generate various scenarios and ask ``What would the scheduler do next?".  
The exact answer will depend very specifically on how the scheduler is 
implemented,  but we
can learn better how to think about schedulers by encountering these situations. 

<*s>

Use on-line PDF scheduler worksheet.

Each Line = 333 $\mu$sec.

Assume a system interrupt every 1,000 $\mu$sec.
Mark interrupts at left edge of every third line.
Each time scheduler runs it requires 100$\mu$sec (approximately 1/3 of a line).

