\subsection*{Delay Functions and Scheduling}

Tasks frequently need to wait for a time interval.

Reasons:
\begin{itemize}
  \item To create time-based events (such as Flash an LED for 0.5 sec).
  \item To wait for something external to happen such as for a device to
           be ready for more data.
  \item To free up CPU time for other processes.
  \item To determine for themselves how often they run:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1)
  {
  compute;
  sleep(20ms);  // Run every ~20ms.
  }
\end{verbatim}
This task sets its own period, $P$, to 20ms.
How accurate?

\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


The worst way to do this is a delay loop such as
\begin{verbatim}
#define DELAY    22000
for (i=0; i< DELAY ; i++) ;   // just loop to use up time
\end{verbatim}

<*chn>

Here we assume that we have figured out that 22000 is the right value to
use for the time delay we want by trial-and-error or calculation.   The
worst bad thing about this method is that it doesn't meet the third
reason, it uses all the CPU time itself.  (There are other problems as
well). We can define
the call {\tt sleep(int delay)} which does the following:
 

<*s>
The scheduler can help implement delays with a function:
{\tt sleep(int delay)}:

<*>
\begin{itemize}
        \item returns to the scheduler
        \item sets a software counter to {\tt delay}.  
        \item schedules the next task.
        \item the task which called {\tt OS\_TimeDelay()} will be set to 
         {\tt Waiting} (not started by the scheduler)
         \item Each tick, decrement {\tt delay} value for each sleeping task. 
        \item when the {\tt delay } is over, set task to {\tt Ready}
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 4.1.4 
\subsubsection{Exercise 4}

\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Assume a time delay function: {\tt sleep(int d)} is now available to the tasks 
within a non-preemptive round robin scheduler.  Tasks take the form:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1) {
  compute for a while;
  sleep(14);  // 14 ms. is just example.
  return;
  }
\end{verbatim} 
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:} 
\begin{itemize}
\item The three tasks are labeled A, B, C
\item The nature of the three tasks is as follows:
    \begin{description}
      \item [A] P=2.0ms, C= 233$\mu$sec, D=1.0ms
      \item [B] P=4.0ms, C= 233$\mu$sec, D=1.0ms, task pseudocode: 
\begin{verbatim}
            taskB(void) {
            compute for 233 mu sec;
            sleep(3);
            return;
            }
\end{verbatim}
      \item [C]  P=1.0ms, C=100$\mu$sec, D=1.0ms  
    \end{description}
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}





\subsection*{Dynamic Scheduling}

<*chn>
\paragraph{Starting and Stopping}
Sometimes it is desireable to make a task stop and start. Recall the task states defined in 
Section \ref{TaskStates}.  We defined the
task state {\tt Inactive}, to describe a stopped task.  To implement
this, assume
that functions are available: {\tt halt\_me()} which moves a task from
{\tt Running} to
{\tt Inactive} states (takes a task out of the
scheduler's loop).  And {\tt run(task)} which moves a task from {\tt
Inactive} to {\tt Ready} (puts a task into the
scheduler loop).

<*s>
Why not allow tasks to start and stop?

Assume two functions:

 {\tt halt\_me().} Moves a task from {\tt Running}
to {\tt Inactive}

 {\tt start(task)}  Moves a task from {\tt Inactive}
 to {\tt Ready}.

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 4.1.5 
\subsubsection{Exercise 5: Scheduling with {\tt halt\_me()} function}

\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Assume a halt function ({\tt halt\_me()}) is now available to the tasks within a non-preemptive round robin scheduler.   Tasks now might look like
\begin{verbatim}

taskA(void *p){
  static int i=0;
  compute a while;
  if(i++ > 2) halt_me();  // turn off after 3 loops
  return;
  }
\end{verbatim} 
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:}  
	\begin{itemize}
	\item The three tasks are labeled A, B, C
	\item The nature of the three tasks is as follows:
		\begin{description}
		\item [A] Intermittent:
		\begin{verbatim}
		 //  see above
		\end{verbatim}
		\item [B] Intermittent:  run for 666ms, then call {\tt sleep()} for 2.0 ms.  Repeat.
		\item [C] Background:
		\begin{verbatim}
		void taskC {
		compute for 0.233 ms; // work in 233usec chunks
		return();
		}
		\end{verbatim}
		\end{description} 
	\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
 
  
\subsubsection*{Scheduling with I/O requests} 
<*chn>
\paragraph{I/O}
Sometimes tasks in embedded systems do I/O by directly manipulating
hardware. For example, a task may set some bits on a digital output
port using the various digital I/O registers in the microcontroller.  These types of operation  take
very little time so they can be appropriate for periodic hard real time tasks.


On the other hand, more sophisticated devices such as  disk drives or network
interfaces require complex driver
software and importantly take significant time to respond to commands.
This type of I/O is typically supported by an embedded operating system
which handles all I/O requests through subroutine calls.  Since tasks
using these devices will have to wait a long time for them to respond, the scheduler
is invoked after I/O requests.  If a task is waiting for I/O, the
scheduler will try to run another task until the I/O is complete.
From a scheduler's point of view, I/O requests are a lot like {\tt sleep(int d)} except {\tt d} is not known in advance (we will specify {\tt d} for schedule diagramming excercises or exam problems). 
<*s>
\begin{itemize}
 \item Bit level I/O is fast and can be done by periodic hard real time tasks.
 \item I/O to devices like disk drives and network interfaces are different.
 \item Complex, slow, variable completion time. 
 \item Requires driver software
 \item Operating System handles the I/O with subroutine calls to driver.
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
 \subsection*{ Example: }
     \begin{itemize}
         \item  After task ``B'' calls for I/O, scheduler runs another task.
         \item  Scheduler starts an I/Otask (runs while task ``B" sleeps).
         \item  I/Otask sets some HW bits and waits for an interrupt. (low CPU)
         \item Task ``B'' will  be re-started by scheduler when requested I/O is finished.
     \end{itemize}
<*>



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 4.1.8 
