\subsubsection*{Foreground/Background}
\begin{itemize}
        \item A mix of hard real-time periodic tasks with
        Soft real time or non real time intermittent and complex background tasks.
        \item ``Foreground" --- the hard real time tasks
        \item ``Background" --- the soft/non real time tasks
        \item Example: Factory Automation Controller:
        \begin{itemize}
                \item  Real time control system (periodic, hard
real-time, forground)
                \item  Hourly production report generator (intermittent, soft-real time, background)
                \item  Inventory Database Update (complex, non-realtime, background)
        \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection{Exercise 7: Foreground/Background}
\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Non-preemptive RR
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:}
\begin{description} 
                \item[A:] Periodic.  $P = 2$msec, $C = 233\mu$sec,
                           $D =500\mu$sec.
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  compute for 0.233ms;
  sleep(1);
  return;
  }
\end{verbatim}
                \item[B:] Complex (background): 
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  compute for 0.233ms;
  return;
  }
\end{verbatim}

\end{description} 



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 5 
\section{Pre-emptive Scheduling}

<*chn>
So far, we have required that tasks return to the scheduler frequently
so that other tasks get a chance to run in the CPU.  This has two
problems.  First, we have to code our tasks somewhat artificially.  They
have to break their job up into chunks and keep track.  Second, we are
vulnerable if some task doesn't ``play fair".

The solution to this is a scheduler that can break in to a running task
using a regular interrupt such as a clock interrupt.   There are many
complex issues in having this ability but the value is very great.
Preemptive schedulers solve this problem.  While a task is running, 
the pre-emptive scheduler can stop it and run something else.

This idea changes how we can write tasks --- for the better! We no longer have to divide our work into bite-size chunks which can fit into a small part of the OS tick and then keep returning.  Instead, we just pretend that we are the only task needing the CPU:
<*s>
\subsubsection*{Pre-emptive Scheduler can}
\begin{itemize}

   \item respond to a clock interrupt during a running task
   \item save context of the interrupted task
   \item look at the entire task list
   \item start another task (or possibly re-start the same task)
\end{itemize}

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<s>\subsubsection*{Writing Tasks for pre-emptive scheduling}
<*>
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  compute;
  }
\end{verbatim}
<chn>The scheduler will now take care of breaking our work into small chunks.  If we have nothing to do, we can use the {\tt sleep()} function to release time to other tasks that we do not need.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<s>If we have nothing to do for a while: sleep!
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  compute;
  sleep(100);
  }
\end{verbatim}
<*chn>
In this case, we do our whole job, and then we wait for 100ms.   We can use this creatively to manage time easily. For example, consider a task to generate two 10ms pulses, 26ms apart, followed by 200ms, then repeat the pulses.   We could write this:

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<s>Example:  Generate a 10ms pulse, wait 26ms, generate a 10ms pulse, wait 200ms, repeat.
<*>
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  Output(PIN, 1) ;   // set the first pulse
  sleep(10);
  Output(PIN, 0) ; 
  sleep(26);
  Output(PIN, 1) ;   // set the second pulse
  sleep(10);
  Output(PIN, 0) ; 
  sleep(200);
  }
\end{verbatim}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsubsection*{Scheduler Types}
Several types of pre-emptive schedulers are described below.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 5.0.1 
\subsubsection*{Types of pre-emptive schedulers:}

<chn>\begin{itemize}

<chn>        \item
Pre-emptive Round Robin
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Regular OS interrupts (``ticks'').
                \item Scheduler moves to next task each tick.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
Task()
{
while{1}
   { compute ; }
}
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>     \item
Priority Based
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Each task assigned priority by programmer
                \item Highest priority task always gets the CPU if
ready.                \item \begin{verbatim}
(same)
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item
 Rate Monotonic (RMS)
        \begin{itemize}
                \item As above but priorities are assigned based on
                period, $P$.
                \item Short period = high priority.
                \item Can prove optimality and schedulability (with
assumptions).      
                \item \begin{verbatim}
(same)
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

RMS Schedulability:

$C_i = $ execution time of task $i$.

$P_i = $ period of task $i$.

$n = $ number of tasks.

if 

\[
\sum^{n-1}_{i=0}\frac{C_i}{P_i} \leq n(2^{1/n}-1)
\]
the system {\bf is} schedulable using RMS.

Assumption:  All tasks pre-emptable 100\% of the time.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item 
Priority Based: How to avoid {\tt starvation}

\begin{quotation}
{\bf starvation}: The case in scheduling where one or more tasks get no CPU time but needs it.
\end{quotation}

        \begin{itemize}
                \item Fixed {\it unique} priority, task-specific delay.
                \item Must tweak delay values, $M_i$,  to avoid
starvation.                \item \begin{verbatim}
Task()
  {
  compute_some();
  OSTimeDelay(Mi);
  }
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>       \item
 Priority Based Round Robin: when each task must run each OS tick.  (because of fast requirements).
        \begin{itemize}
                \item All tasks delay 1 tick
                \item All tasks execute $< \frac{T}{N}$ where $T$ is the
                OS tick period and $N$ is the number of tasks.
                \item  \begin{verbatim}
Task()
  {
  compute_a_little();
  OSTimeDelay(1);
  }
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<chn> \item
FreeRTOS Style Priority-Based. 
             \begin{itemize}
                \item 7 priority levels
		\item RR within each level.
              \end{itemize}

<chn>\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 5.0.2 
\subsubsection*{Preemptive Scheduling Exercises}

\subsubsection{Exercise 8: Preemptive Priority Based Scheduling}

\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Preemptive Priority Based. Priority order: A=2, B=1, C=3. (1= highest)
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:}  
            \begin{description} 
    \item [A] Intermittent: Run for 2ms and then {\tt halt\_me()}.
    \item [B] Complex:  run for 1.0ms, then call the OS to do I/O which
      lasts for 2.0 ms.  Repeat.
    \item [C] Background: Run forever.
\end{description}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection{Exercise 9: Preemptive Round Robin Scheduling}

\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Preemptive RR (no priorities)
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:}  
            \begin{description} 
    \item [A] Intermittent: Run for 2ms and then {\tt halt\_me()}.
    \item [B] Complex:  run for 1.0ms, then call the OS to do I/O which
      lasts for 2.0 ms.  Repeat.
    \item [C] Background: Run forever.
\end{description}
  


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection{Exercise 10: Pre-emptive RR Scheduling}

\paragraph{Scheduler Type:} Preemptive RR (no priorities)
\paragraph{Task List and Characteristics:}  
            \begin{description} 
               \item 5 tasks. For each one P = 20ms, C=3ms, D=20ms. 
  	       \item OS time tick is 1ms.
            \end{description}

How can you set up priorities (each task gets a different priority) and delay values to meet their schedules?
 

<s>\end{slide}
